  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTemperatures early this afternoon have warmed into the mid to\nupper 70s inland of Lake Michigan under clear skies. Essentially a\nrinse and repeat of yesterday, where several locations are already\nwithin a few degrees of record highs with several hours of warming\nand mixing yet to occur. Expect additional records to be broken by\nlate this afternoon, and again Monday afternoon. Have continued\nthe trend of blending in NBM 90th percentile to nudge highs up by\na couple degrees. Less confidence in highs being broken Monday \nafternoon since there will be some mid clouds around, though there\nwill be decent windows for clearing. Otherwise, southerly winds \nremain breezy (though not as strong as yesterday), with obs \nshowing gusts to around 20 mph.\n\nPrecip chances... CAMs have trended slightly earlier with the \narrival of light rain on Monday, with the cold front now \nencroaching on north-central Wisconsin early Monday morning. As \nsuch, some models generate light QPF over the north Monday morning\nalong the front. However, the main push of precip (50 to 80% \nchance) looks to move through late Monday afternoon through Monday\nnight with support from transient upper shortwave. Front still \nlooks moisture-starved for the most part, so probabilities for \nreceiving 0.25\" of rain remain at a meager 20 to 50% north of Hwy \n29. Embedded non-severe storms remain possible as corridor of \nelevated instability (~600 to 800 J/kg) develops along the front, \nthough any convective activity would remain elevated.\n\nNo further clarity provided for end of week system as ensemble\nclusters remain divided on synoptic pattern evolution. As it so\noften is, phasing potential remains a major source of uncertainty\nas some models show northern and southern streams phasing over \nthe Great Lakes, while some show this occurring further east.\nAs it stands now, still looks on track to bring us our next \nwidespread precip chances, though this is subject to change as\nmodels hopefully get a better handle on the upper pattern. \nConsistent signal also remains for the first flakes of the season \nmixing in across the north along the back edge of the system.\n\nOutlook... More fall-like pattern sets up at the end of the week \nas arctic high brings below normal temperatures from the Plains to\nthe Midwest. 850 mb temperatures will tank to well below 0C as\nnorthwest flow ramps up, resulting in high temperatures reading in\nthe 50s for most. Frost/freeze setup looks likely from central to\neast-central Wisconsin as we approach the end of the growing\nseason.",
